Q3 – Engineer the hashCode–equals Contract

1. Introduction
In a college fest, student ID cards are scanned at different entry gates. The same student may be scanned multiple times.

To avoid duplicate attendance records, Java provides the HashSet collection.

equals() checks whether two objects are logically equal.

hashCode() generates an integer hash value used to identify the bucket where an object should be stored.

Important contract: If two objects are equal according to equals(), they must return the same hash code.

In the given Student class, equals() is overridden, but hashCode() is not overridden. This can cause duplicate student entries in a HashSet.

2. Three Designed Tests and Predictions
Test 1 – Different Students
Input:
present.add(new Student("192311001", "Arun"));
present.add(new Student("192311002", "Divya"));
Prediction: Set size = 2.

Reason: Both students have different registration numbers, so they are considered different students.
Test 2 – Same Student Scanned Twice
Input:
present.add(new Student("192311001", "Arun"));
present.add(new Student("192311001", "Arun"));
Prediction before fixing hashCode(): The set may contain 2 objects because the default hashCode() may give different hash values to the two objects. HashSet may place them in different buckets without checking equals().

Prediction after fixing hashCode(): Set size = 1 because both objects have the same registration number and hash code.
Test 3 – Same Registration Number, Different Name
Input:
present.add(new Student("192311003", "Kumar"));
present.add(new Student("192311003", "Rahul"));
Prediction after fixing hashCode(): Set size = 1.

Reason: The equals() method compares only registration numbers. Therefore, even though the names are different, both objects represent the same student according to the defined equality rule.
